In comparison to an IBM PC clone or a modern day MAC or a (non-terminal)
sun system yes. Back then there was different differences between home
computers and game consoles: no ram to speak of, only TV output, 16
colours only for graphics. As modern computers became expandable game
consoles were (and always will be due to price) less so. You can't
compare a atari game machine from the 80's to a MARK I either, the
comparison is meaningless. I had a C64 back then and there were
*significant* limitations to the extent that I would be tempted to call
it just a game machine now: No real disk operating system (you "talked"
directly to the drive), only Basic/ML. Cartidge games - which you
haven't seen since the ADAM in computers. 

